      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we can use the arc length formula for parametric curves. The formula for the arc length \\(L\\) of the curve parameterized by \\((x(t), y(t))\\) from \\(t = a\\) to \\(t = b\\) is given by:\n\n\\[L = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\\]\n\nFor the given parametric equations \\(x = 2 \\sin t\\) and \\(y = 2 \\cos t\\), we need to find:\n\n1. \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\)\n2. \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\)\n3. Substitute \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\) into the arc length formula.\n\nLet's calculate step by step.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable t\nt = sp.symbols('t')\n\n# Parametric equations\nx = 2 * sp.sin(t)\ny = 2 * sp.cos(t)\n\n# Compute the derivatives with respect to t\ndx_dt = sp.diff(x, t)\ndy_dt = sp.diff(y, t)\n\n# Compute the integrand of the arc length formula\nintegrand = sp.sqrt(dx_dt**2 + dy_dt**2)\n\n# Integrate to find the arc length from t = 0 to t = pi\narc_length = sp.integrate(integrand, (t, 0, sp.pi))\nprint(arc_length.evalf())\n```\n```output\n11.7851932716923\n```\nThe length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is \\(\\boxed{11.7851932716923}\\).",
