      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\)from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the length formula for parametric curves:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(a = 0\\) and \\(b = \\pi\\).\n\nFirst, let's find the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[\nx(t) = 2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\\[\ny(t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\) into the length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left(2 \\cos t\\right)^2 + \\left(-2 \\sin t\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nFactor out 4 from the square root:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nRecall the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4 \\cdot 1} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify further:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nIntegrate \\(2dt\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi}\n\\]\n\nEvaluate the definite integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
