      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "From the given parametric equations, we can discern that $x$ and $y$ describe the circle centered at the origin with radius 2. In other words, $(x,y) = (r \\sin t, r \\cos t)$ and\n\\begin{align*}\nr &= x^2 + y^2 \\\\\n&= (2 \\sin t)^2 + (2 \\cos t)^2 \\\\\n&= 6 \\sin^2 t.\n\\end{align*}Similarly,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{dr}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(6 \\sin^2 t) \\\\\n&= 12 \\sin t \\cos t.\n\\end{align*}Next, we want to find $\\frac{dr}{dx}$. Recall that\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{dr}{dx} &= \\frac{\\frac{dr}{dt} (dx/dt)}{\\frac{dx}{dt}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\frac{12 \\sin t \\cos t}{dt} (2 \\sin t)}{2 \\sin t} \\text{ (we plugged in } dx/dt = 2\\cos t)\\\\\n&= \\frac{6 \\cos^2 t}{1}.\n\\end{align*}Now we can evaluate $\\int_0^\\pi \\left( \\tfrac{dr}{dx} \\right) dt$. Since $\\tfrac{dr}{dx} = 6 \\cos^2 t$, we can write\n\\begin{align*}\n\\int_0^\\pi \\left( \\tfrac{dr}{dx} \\right) dt &= \\int_0^\\pi \\left( 6 \\cos^2 t \\right) dt \\\\\n&= 6 \\int_0^\\pi \\left( \\frac{1 + \\cos 2t}{2} \\right) dt \\\\\n&= \\boxed{6} .\n\\end{align*}Comments:\nRecall the double angle formula for cosine: $\\cos^2 t = \\cos^2 t - \\sin^2 t$.",
